{"id":"workloads-controllers-daemonset","corpus":"k8s","title":"DaemonSet","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/daemonset/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"DaemonSet"},{"kind":"p","text":"A DaemonSet defines Pods that provide node-local facilities. These might be fundamental to the operation of your cluster, such as a networking helper tool, or be part of an add-on."},{"kind":"p","text":"A DaemonSet ensures that all (or some) Nodes run a copy of a Pod. As nodes are added to the cluster, Pods are added to them. As nodes are removed from the cluster, those Pods are garbage collected. Deleting a DaemonSet will clean up the Pods it created."},{"kind":"p","text":"Some typical uses of a DaemonSet are:"},{"kind":"li","text":"running a cluster storage daemon on every node"},{"kind":"li","text":"running a logs collection daemon on every node"},{"kind":"li","text":"running a node monitoring daemon on every node"},{"kind":"p","text":"In a simple case, one DaemonSet, covering all nodes, would be used for each type of daemon. A more complex setup might use multiple DaemonSets for a single type of daemon, but with different flags and/or different memory and cpu requests for different hardware types."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Writing a DaemonSet Spec"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Create a DaemonSet"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can describe a DaemonSet in a YAML file. For example, the daemonset.yaml file below describes a DaemonSet that runs the fluentd-elasticsearch Docker image:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Create a DaemonSet based on the YAML file:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Required Fields"},{"kind":"p","text":"As with all other Kubernetes config, a DaemonSet needs apiVersion, kind, and metadata fields. For general information about working with config files, see running stateless applications and object management using kubectl."},{"kind":"p","text":"The name of a DaemonSet object must be a valid DNS subdomain name."},{"kind":"p","text":"A DaemonSet also needs a .spec section."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Pod Template"},{"kind":"p","text":"The .spec.template is one of the required fields in .spec."},{"kind":"p","text":"The .spec.template is a pod template. It has exactly the same schema as a Pod, except it is nested and does not have an apiVersion or kind."},{"kind":"p","text":"In addition to required fields for a Pod, a Pod template in a DaemonSet has to specify appropriate labels (see pod selector)."},{"kind":"p","text":"A Pod Template in a DaemonSet must have a RestartPolicy equal to Always, or be unspecified, which defaults to Always."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Pod Selector"},{"kind":"p","text":"The .spec.selector field is a pod selector. It works the same as the .spec.selector of a Job."},{"kind":"p","text":"You must specify a pod selector that matches the labels of the .spec.template. Also, once a DaemonSet is created, its .spec.selector can not be mutated. Mutating the pod selector can lead to the unintentional orphaning of Pods, and it was found to be confusing to users."},{"kind":"p","text":"The .spec.selector is an object consisting of two fields:"},{"kind":"li","text":"matchLabels - works the same as the .spec.selector of a ReplicationController."},{"kind":"li","text":"matchExpressions - allows to build more sophisticated selectors by specifying key, list of values and an operator that relates the key and values."},{"kind":"p","text":"When the two are specified the result is ANDed."},{"kind":"p","text":"The .spec.selector must match the .spec.template.metadata.labels. Config with these two not matching will be rejected by the API."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Running Pods on select Nodes"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you specify a .spec.template.spec.nodeSelector, then the DaemonSet controller will create Pods on nodes which match that node selector. Likewise if you specify a .spec.template.spec.affinity, then DaemonSet controller will create Pods on nodes which match that node affinity. If you do not specify either, then the DaemonSet controller will create Pods on all nodes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How Daemon Pods are scheduled"},{"kind":"p","text":"A DaemonSet can be used to ensure that all eligible nodes run a copy of a Pod. The DaemonSet controller creates a Pod for each eligible node and adds the spec.affinity.nodeAffinity field of the Pod to match the target host. After the Pod is created, the default scheduler typically takes over and then binds the Pod to the target host by setting the .spec.nodeName field. If the new Pod cannot fit on the node, the default scheduler may preempt (evict) some of the existing Pods based on the priority of the new Pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"If it's important that the DaemonSet pod run on each node, it's often desirable to set the .spec.template.spec.priorityClassName of the DaemonSet to a PriorityClass with a higher priority to ensure that this eviction occurs."},{"kind":"p","text":"The user can specify a different scheduler for the Pods of the DaemonSet, by setting the .spec.template.spec.schedulerName field of the DaemonSet."}],"lemmas":{"defines":["defines","define"],"defining":["defining","define"],"defined":["defined","define"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"provides":["provides","provide"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"locals":["local","locals"],"facilities":["facility","facilities"],"fundamentals":["fundamental","fundamentals"],"operations":["operation","operations"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"networking":["networking","network"],"networkings":["networkings","networking"],"networks":["network","networks"],"helpers":["helper","helpers"],"tools":["tool","tools"],"tooling":["tooling","tool"],"parts":["part","parts"],"parted":["parted","part"],"parting":["parting","part"],"adding":["adding","add"],"ensures